# What must never enter a build context.
#
# `deploy/docker/Dockerfile` and `Dockerfile.web` both `COPY . .`, and the runtime stage of the
# first copies `/app/apps` and `/app/packages` wholesale. Without this file the developer's own
# `dist/` rides along into the image — and because Docker preserves mtimes, `tsc -b` then finds
# every project up to date and compiles nothing, so what ships is whatever the host happened to
# have built, from whatever branch it was on.
#
# `node_modules` is the other half: it is 240 MB of the 256 MB context, it overwrites the
# `deps` stage's lockfile install (defeating the layer caching that stage exists for), and it
# carries the host's platform, which is wrong the moment anyone builds for another architecture.

node_modules/
**/node_modules/
**/dist/
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Built by `pnpm --filter @saga/cli bundle` inside the image. A host copy would be served at
# /api/cli/saga in its place, which is the one artifact that must match the server exactly.
apps/cli/pack/

# The quickstart writes a real session secret and a real bootstrap admin password in here
# (README step 2) before running `docker compose up -d --build`. Compose reads `.env` from the
# host for interpolation; nothing in the image ever needs the file itself.
.env
.env.*

# Test and run artifacts. Large, local, and meaningless inside an image.
test-results/
playwright-report/
.saga-run/
.saga/

# `.git` is deliberately NOT excluded: `apps/cli/scripts/bundle.mjs` reads the commit from it to
# stamp the CLI build, and without it every image would serve `0.1.0+local.<wall clock>` instead
# of `0.1.0+g<sha>`. It is 2.7 MB and reaches no runtime stage.
